ZEBRA = """Honk! In Animal City, the roads were busy, but every street crossed itself with painted stripes that could walk. Emily was the morning crossing helper, holding her big yellow lollipop sign. She had one simple job: keep the white stripe flat until the ducks walked by. But Officer Waddle, a wide-footed pelican, dropped a heavy bag of birdseed right on the stripe's ticklish middle. The zebra crossing giggled, curled its edges, and trotted away down the avenue! Emily gasped. She had to catch that stripe before the school rush started.

Emily sprinted down Sunny Boulevard, waving her bright lollipop sign in the air. Tap-tap-tap went the giggling crosswalk, galloping on painted corners past fruit carts and sleepy foxes. "Wait! Come back!" Emily called out. Behind her, Officer Waddle tripped over his enormous webbed feet. "Waddle, squawk, flap!" he squawked, puffing wildly. The pelican lunged forward, snapped his wide beak shut, and caught the bottom of Emily's jacket. He tried to pull the brakes, shouting, "Stop that stripe!" Together, they skidded along the shiny road, pulled right along by the ticklish runaway.

Across the street, the giant town hall clock ticked loudly. Tick, tock! The big hand moved closer to school time. A line of fluffy ducks waited on the pavement, looking confused. Emily and Officer Waddle bumped along the road. The pelican tripped over his big webbed feet again. "Waddle, squawk, flap!" he cried. He fell forward, and his heavy pelican beak pouch caught right on the runaway stripe's tail! Now he was stuck to the bouncing white paper line. Emily held onto his jacket. Together, they all zoomed past a fruit cart, shouting, "Stop that stripe!"

The giggling stripe looped around a tall green lamppost like a shiny ribbon. Swoosh! It curled back across the street and scooped Officer Waddle right onto its striped back. The pelican wobbled wildly, his big wings flapping in the wind. He grabbed Emily's hand tightly to keep from falling. Now the ticklish paper crosswalk galloped faster and faster, whipping them both around the bend. It bounced right over a puddle and raced toward the school corner. Officer Waddle flapped his wings and yelled, "Stop that stripe!"

The runaway zebra crossing reached the school corner and started to spin. It spun in wild, dizzy circles! "Waddle, squawk, flap!" squawked Officer Waddle. His huge webbed feet got all tangled up. He tripped and tumbled right off the paper stripe's back. He rolled safely across the road and landed in a pile of soft leaves. Now Emily was all alone in the street. The giggling paper stripe stopped spinning. It curled its edges up like a galloping pony. Then it pointed straight at Emily and charged right toward her!

Emily planted her shoes firmly on the shiny road. The giggling zebra crossing galloped closer and closer, kicking up painted dust. Emily held her breath. She gripped the pole of her big yellow lollipop sign with both hands. She thrust the round sign high into the bright morning air, standing right in its path. "STOP!" Emily shouted with all her might. The galloping paper stripe saw the bright yellow sign blocking the way, gasped, and skidded to a sudden halt on the tarmac.

The runaway crosswalk knew that big yellow sign meant business. It stopped giggling instantly. With a soft rustle, it unrolled itself completely flat on the street, exactly where it belonged. Officer Waddle stood up from the leaves and dusted off his heavy wings. "Good job, Emily!" he squawked. Across the road, the fluffy ducks saw the flat white stripes. They waddled happily onto the paper, tap-dancing safely across the street to school. Emily lowered her bright lollipop sign. She stood in the bright sunshine and smiled proudly as the school bell rang."""
JESS = """In the tall palace nursery, Princess Jess had a big job. A fat puppy sat on her head! This puppy thought he was a golden crown. He gave a tiny bark. "Yap, yap, nap!" sang Jess. But crowns do not belong in the nursery. This crown wanted his warm basket downstairs.

Princess Jess picked up a soft velvet cushion. She tucked it right under her arm. "Stay still, royal crown," she whispered. The puppy gave a sleepy sniff. Jess took a slow, grand tip-toe out of the nursery door. Softly, softly! If she walked too fast, the royal crown might slip.

Jess reached the top of the grand stairs. Down below stood the tall hall clock. Its big brass hands ticked and tocked. It was getting closer and closer to the bedtime gong! "Yap, yap, nap," snored the puppy crown. If the loud gong rang, the puppy would wake up grumpy. Jess had to hurry, but she stepped very, very carefully.

She reached the bottom step and stepped onto the shiny hall floor. Suddenly, the puppy crown stretched his little paws. He leaned hard to the side for a giant yawn. Oh no! Jess wobbled left. She wobbled right. She shook like a royal jelly on a plate. The puppy was slipping down her ear!

Jess bent her knees quickly. She caught her balance just in time. Then she froze as still as a stone statue. The hall was completely quiet. Slowly, the puppy crown pulled his paws in. He shuffled around and settled perfectly upright on her curls again. Jess let out a long, quiet breath. They were safe.

Jess crept to the warm basket in the corner. How could she get the puppy off her head? She remembered the soft velvet cushion! Jess held the cushion out right beneath her chin. Then she took a slow, deep princess bow. The sleepy puppy crown slid smoothly off her curls. He landed safely on the velvet.

Princess Jess set the soft velvet cushion into the warm basket. The puppy curled into a round, sleepy ball with a happy sigh. His ears flopped down, and his tail stopped wagging. "Yap, yap, nap," Jess sang softly. The bedtime clock struck its gong, but the royal puppy crown was already fast asleep."""
